# q79 space-5 fixed-u1 D-augmented finite-cover theorem

Date: 2026-07-20

## Setup

Work in either inverse-root scalar representative chart `s=1,2` for mirror
space 5 over `F_101`. Fix `u1=1`. For every `a,v` in `F_101^*`, the two
endpoint equations force

```text
u0 = 1,
u2 = s*a^(-2),
u3 = a*v^(-1).
```

Let `I_R(s,a,v)` be the ideal generated by parent rows 1 through 12 after
these substitutions and after eliminating the four variables absent from
those rows, `y1,...,y4`. Let `I_full(s,a,v)` be the ideal generated by rows
1 through 12 and 14 through 21 with every `y` variable retained. The omitted
rows 0 and 13 are the endpoint equations already made identically zero by
the displayed substitutions.

## Theorem

For every

```text
s in {1,2},  a in F_101^*,  v in F_101^*,
```

the complete selected R/y/D fiber ideal is the unit ideal. Equivalently, the
space-5, `u1=1` selected system has no point on its full 20,000-element
nonzero `F_101` endpoint grid.

More precisely, the frozen exact Groebner-basis cover has the following
partition:

```text
                         R-only [1]    full R/y/D [1]    total
scalar class 1               4,998                 2     5,000
scalar class 2               4,995                 5     5,000
canonical a=1,...,50         9,993                 7    10,000
after sign involution       19,986                14    20,000
```

The seven canonical fallback fibers are

```text
(s,a,v) = (1,18,56), (1,50,93),
          (2,2,86), (2,5,58), (2,14,18), (2,16,10), (2,35,10).
```

At every other canonical fiber, the embedded exact msolve output is the
literal one-element reduced Groebner basis `[1]` for `I_R`. Since `I_R` is a
subideal of the complete substituted parent ideal, this already excludes the
full fiber. At each of the seven displayed fibers, a second exact solve keeps
all four `y` variables and adds all four D-terminal rows; its embedded output
is the literal reduced basis `[1]` for `I_full`.

Thus every canonical fiber is excluded by an explicit unit basis. No timeout,
incomplete output, numerical residual, or nonunit solver output is promoted
to a no-solution claim.

## Sign completion

The separately proved parent involution

```text
(a,v) -> (-a,-v)
```

fixes all 22 parent polynomials and the forced values of `u2` and `u3`. It
maps every canonical representative `a=1,...,50` bijectively to its omitted
partner `101-a`, while `v=1,...,100` is permuted bijectively. Therefore the
10,000 canonical calculations imply the other 10,000 endpoint fibers without
new solves.

Each displayed fixed fiber is a unit ideal over `F_101`, so that particular
fiber also stays empty after any field extension. The finite enumeration,
however, ranges only over `a,v in F_101^*`; it is not a symbolic proof for
extension-valued endpoint parameters.

## Reproducible certificate

The proof freezes 100 complete line packets, each containing all 100 nonzero
`v` outcomes, and five full-parent packets containing the seven fallback
witnesses. The deterministic certifier checks:

1. the exact parent hashes and row selections;
2. every forced endpoint coordinate by modular inversion;
3. the ordered 100-value `v` exhaustion in every line packet;
4. a literal reduced basis `[1]` in all 9,993 R-only closures;
5. a literal reduced basis `[1]` in all seven full R/y/D fallbacks;
6. the exact sign-involution certificate and its finite exhaustion; and
7. zero added continuous fit parameters.

The resulting certificate is

```text
certificates/Q79_Ronly_FixedU1_Space5_D_Augmented_Cover_v1.json
```

## Claim boundary

This is an exact finite-field endpoint-grid theorem for mirror space 5 at
`u1=1`, across both scalar square-class representatives. It closes two of the
400 `(space, scalar class, u1)` solver slices needed by the present finite
cover strategy.

It does not close `u1=2,...,100` in space 5, any nonzero `u1` in space 6, the
two remaining mirror charts as symbolic schemes, extension-valued endpoint
parameters, or a characteristic-zero lift. The global chart accounting
therefore remains `138/140`. It also does not promote the finite polynomial
source to selected physical HYM or quantum-gravity data.

No continuous fit parameter is introduced.

